/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "131c418a-0f72-5d16-9338-5a4fe94769a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438404} :answer-field/id #uuid "43b521a3-f637-5d01-b797-9ec7e2c91ada" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e687e18c-9edd-57ea-b75b-3cb3764012f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-66514" :question/problem "![](images/f6/f65062d48189401ba22b1b08637d187eed532d5f93dca7663c2e80743510d5d7.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](images/cc/cc31f6f291058c7890a9210d5ebfac21cdd961c74c3db5e080d7fca334102b53.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that: \n\n- For graph III, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph III shows a one-to-one function. This means that the function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, II and IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"}]}
